WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.599 --> 00:00:04.720
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:09.110
Image denoising is an area where we have a
noisy image as an input, and we wish to get

00:00:09.110 --> 00:00:11.620
a clear, noise-free image.

00:00:11.620 --> 00:00:15.920
Neural network-based solutions are amazing
at this, because we can feed them a large

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:19.910
amount of training data with noisy inputs
and clear outputs.

00:00:19.910 --> 00:00:24.450
And if we do that, during the training process,
the neural network will be able to learn the

00:00:24.450 --> 00:00:30.119
concept of noise, and when presented with
a new, previously unseen noisy image, it will

00:00:30.119 --> 00:00:32.149
be able to clear it up.

00:00:32.149 --> 00:00:36.949
However, with light transport simulations,
creating a noisy image means following the

00:00:36.949 --> 00:00:43.239
path of millions and millions of light rays,
which can take up to hours per training sample.

00:00:43.239 --> 00:00:47.389
And we need thousands, or potentially hundreds
of thousands of these!

00:00:47.389 --> 00:00:51.569
There are also other cases where creating
the clean images for the training set is not

00:00:51.569 --> 00:00:54.839
just expensive, but flat out impossible.

00:00:54.839 --> 00:01:00.749
Low-light photography, astronomical imaging,
or magnetic resonance imaging, MRI in short

00:01:00.749 --> 00:01:02.850
are great examples of this.

00:01:02.850 --> 00:01:07.690
In these cases, we cannot use our neural networks
simply because we cannot build such a training

00:01:07.690 --> 00:01:10.979
set as we don't have access to the clear images.

00:01:10.979 --> 00:01:16.659
In this collaboration between NVIDIA, Aalto
University and MIT, scientists came up with

00:01:16.659 --> 00:01:22.740
an insane idea: let's try to train a neural
network without clear images and use only

00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:23.969
noisy data.

00:01:23.969 --> 00:01:29.080
Normally, we would say that this is clearly
impossible and end this research project.

00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:34.200
However, they show that under a suitable set
of constraints, for instance, one reasonable

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:39.460
assumption about the distribution of the noise
opens up the possibility of restoring noisy

00:01:39.460 --> 00:01:42.710
signals without seeing clean ones.

00:01:42.710 --> 00:01:48.409
This is an insane idea that actually works,
and can help us restore images with significant

00:01:48.409 --> 00:01:50.210
outlier content.

00:01:50.210 --> 00:01:55.789
Not only that, but it is also shown that this
technique can do close to or just as well

00:01:55.789 --> 00:01:59.850
as other previously known techniques that
have access to clean images.

00:01:59.850 --> 00:02:04.810
You can look at these images, many of which
have many different kinds of noise, like camera

00:02:04.810 --> 00:02:10.780
noise, noise from light transport simulations,
MRI imaging, and images severely corrupted

00:02:10.780 --> 00:02:13.000
with a ton of random text.

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:18.400
The usual limitations apply, in short, it
of course cannot possibly recover content

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:20.880
if we cut out a bigger region from our images.

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:26.180
This severely hamstrung training process can
be compared to a regular neural denoiser that

00:02:26.180 --> 00:02:31.320
has access to the clean images, and the differences
are negligible most of the time.

00:02:31.320 --> 00:02:37.140
So how about that, we can teach a neural network
to denoise without ever showing it the concept

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:38.410
of denoising.

00:02:38.410 --> 00:02:42.960
Just the thought of this boggles my mind so
much it keeps me up at night.

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:47.940
This is such a remarkable concept - I hope
there will soon be followup papers that extend

00:02:47.940 --> 00:02:50.340
this idea to other problems as well.

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:54.930
